  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The analysis is limited to rail-transit commuters within a single Chinese megacity, which may not represent extreme commuting patterns for other transport modes or smaller urban contexts.",
      "what": "This study analyzed extreme rail-transit commutes lasting 60 minutes or more during morning and evening peak hours in Shanghai, China, using large-scale smart card data from September 2021. By combining Poisson regression and gradient boosting decision trees, the researchers modeled origin-destination pairs to identify nonlinear associations and threshold effects of built environment variables on extreme commuting.",
      "why": "The findings demonstrate a symmetrical but distinct pattern between morning origin-side variables and evening destination-side variables, proving that extreme rail commuting is jointly driven by the spatial mismatch of living costs, workplace accessibility, and local neighborhood conditions."
    }
